{"id":"9f40e683-73c4-4679-aed9-e33e6d0f248d","object":"chat.completion","created":1784999075,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    # Build maps\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children_map = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n['parent']\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children_map[p].append(n['id'])\\n    # Initial retained set\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    # Verify required symbols exist initially\\n    symbols_in_tree = {n['symbol'] for n in nodes}\\n    if not set(required_symbols).issubset(symbols_in_tree):\\n        raise ValueError('Required symbols not all present in tree')\\n    # Processing order: depth descending, then id ascending\\n    order = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed_node_ids = []\\n    for n in order:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        # Cannot remove if any child is still retained\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children_map[nid]):\\n            continue\\n        # Simulate removal\\n        retained.discard(nid)\\n        # Check if all required symbols still represented\\n        present_symbols = {node_map[rid]['symbol'] for rid in retained}\\n        if not set(required_symbols).issubset(present_symbols):\\n            # Revert and skip\\n            retained.add(nid)\\n            continue\\n        # Successful removal\\n        removed_node_ids.append(nid)\\n    retained_node_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed_node_ids, 'retained_node_ids': retained_node_ids}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` according to the given specification. The problem: grammar-guided node reduction on a parse tree. The case contains nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Required symbols must be preserved. We need to produce removed_node_ids (ordered list of node IDs removed in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted list? The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The output schema requires arrays of strings. The contract says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (presumably ascending). The removal order is the order we decide to remove nodes.\n\nAlgorithm: We have a tree. Nodes are removable or not. Root is always not removable. Required symbols: list of symbols that must remain present in the tree after reduction. We need to remove nodes tentatively if they are removable and their removal does not cause any required symbol to become absent. Also we need to consider children: we cannot remove a node that has retained children (i.e., if any child is retained, we cannot remove the parent? Actually \"never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if a node has any child that is retained, the parent cannot be removed. So removal propagates from leaves upward? We need to process deeper nodes before shallower nodes (i.e., post-order). At equal depth, use node ID as tie-break (stable). We need to \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That means: before removing a node, we need to check if removing it would cause any required symbol to be missing from the tree. The tree after removal must still contain at least one node with each required symbol.\n\nSince we are removing nodes one by one, we need to simulate the tree state. We'll start with the full set of nodes. We'll process nodes in order: deeper depth first (larger depth first), and if depth equal, tie-break by node ID (lexicographic? Stable, probably string compare). For each node, if it is removable, and it has no children that are currently retained (i.e., if any child is still in the retained set, skip), and if removing it does not cause any required symbol to disappear (i.e., there is another node with that symbol somewhere else in retained set after removal), then we remove it. We add its ID to removed_node_ids in order of removal. After removing, we update the retained set.\n\nFinally, retained_node_ids are the IDs of nodes that were never removed. We need to sort them (ascending) as per \"Return sorted retained IDs\".\n\nKey: The removal order is the order of actual removal, which matches the order we process nodes (since we remove as we go). The provided description says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we sort nodes by depth descending, then node ID ascending (string compare? Probably lexicographic). That will be the processing order.\n\nBut note: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That means we process nodes with larger depth first. That's post-order. Good.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Read case payload. It has \"nodes\" (list of dict) and \"required_symbols\" (list of strings). Also has \"_case_nonce\" but not needed.\n2. Build a mapping from node id to node dict. Also build a mapping from node id to list of child ids (by scanning nodes and checking parent). Also keep track of root (parent null).\n3. Build a set of all node IDs initially as retained.\n4. Determine required symbols set. Ensure that initially all required symbols exist in the tree (otherwise infeasible? but specification says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" Maybe just check that required symbols are present initially. If not, raise ValueError.)\n5. Create a list of node IDs sorted by depth descending, and then by id ascending (stable tie). We can use sorted() with key lambda: (-depth, id). Since id is string, compare directly.\n6. For each node ID in that order:\n   - Check if node is removable (removable=True). If not, skip.\n   - Check if node has any children that are still in retained set. We have the child list. For each child, if child id is in retained set, then skip (cannot remove parent with retained children).\n   - Check if removing this node would cause any required symbol to go missing. To do that, we need to simulate: temporarily remove the node from retained set, check if for each required symbol there is still at least one node in retained set with that symbol. If yes, then proceed with removal. Else skip.\n   - If all conditions pass, remove the node: add its id to removed_node_ids, and remove from retained set.\n7. After processing all nodes, compute retained_node_ids as sorted list of the IDs in retained set.\n8. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}.\n\nEdge cases: Nodes that are not removable (removable=false) are never removed. Also root is not removable (given). Required symbols: they must be present after removal. The \"never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" But required symbols are symbols, not nodes. So we should not remove any node that is the only provider of a required symbol. But if a required symbol appears in multiple nodes, we could remove some of them as long as at least one remains. So we need to check the condition based on symbol presence.\n\nImplementation details: We'll need to count occurrences of each required symbol in the retained set. But for efficiency, we can just compute the set of symbols present after tentative removal and compare to required set. Since the tree is small, we can just iterate over retained nodes.\n\nPotential bug: The condition \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" should be checked after removal, but we also need to ensure that the node itself is not the sole representative of a required symbol. So we must check after removing it.\n\nAlso note: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if a node has children that are still retained, we cannot remove it. That means we process leaves first, then internal nodes. Since we process deepest first, and if children are removed first, the parent may become a leaf and then be removable. So the order is correct: after children removed, parent may be considered later (since depth smaller). So we should process all nodes in depth descending order (which is post-order). So we will process leaves first. That's fine.\n\nBut what about nodes that are not removable? They are skipped.\n\nTie-breaking with node ID: stable, so we need to sort by depth descending, then node ID ascending lexicographically. We'll use default string compare.\n\nNow, ensure that we do not mutate the input: we only read from it.\n\nNow, we need to produce Python source code as a JSON string inside {\"implementation\": \"<escaped python>\"}. The function must be named `solve(case)`. Return JSON-serializable dict.\n\nWe also need to handle potential infeasibility: If required symbols missing initially, raise ValueError. Also if after removal we cannot satisfy? Already handled by checking condition.\n\nNow, produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":1936,"total_tokens":4105,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1519},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}